  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Millstone Township incorporated in 1844, named after the Millstone River that rises there, but its roots reach back further.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.savinghistorywithfriends.org/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By 1940, Millstone was more than a stop on the creek; it had a thriving coal camp with a loading tipple and miners’ housing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/millstone-letcher-county-from-craftsville-to-a-coal-camp-and-sewing-center/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community sits at the mouth of Millstone Creek on the North Fork of the Kentucky River, a few miles northeast of Whitesburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/millstone-letcher-county-from-craftsville-to-a-coal-camp-and-sewing-center/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the War on Poverty era, local seamstresses formed the Millstone Sewing Center, repurposing donated clothing to remake garments for needy children.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/millstone-letcher-county-from-craftsville-to-a-coal-camp-and-sewing-center/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Oral histories describe Millstone as a strict mining community where company rules governed daily life, and later unionization reshaped the workforce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
